WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.399
One moved bridge. Every home supplied on time.
LiveWorld turns a small game

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:10.797
world into an experiment, with changes you can see
and results you can

00:00:10.797 --> 00:00:11.213
verify.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.315
A cup becomes a well, blocks become homes, and sticks
become bridges. This

00:00:18.315 --> 00:00:20.258
tabletop image is generated.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.850
Muse Spark proposes the objects and their roles. SAM
traces their shapes. These

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:33.700
are actual returned masks. The creator reviews the
suggestions before the image-relative positions

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.500
become a fixed-grid level.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.295
Now switch to an authored challenge. Six couriers
supply six homes, each with

00:00:42.295 --> 00:00:48.589
its own deadline. Everything is connected, yet four
water units arrive late. Movement,

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:52.947
stock, queues and deadlines are computed by the
simulator.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.319
Balanced dispatch sounds like a reasonable fix. In
this recorded Meta experiment, it

00:01:00.319 --> 00:01:06.639
makes the outcome worse: only fourteen of twenty-four
units arrive on time. The

00:01:06.639 --> 00:01:11.500
simulator rejects the hypothesis. Model confidence
cannot change that result.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.048
The playtester uses simulation tools to investigate
allowed edits. Its search includes a

00:01:18.048 --> 00:01:24.096
systematic sweep; that part is code, not invented
reasoning. Moving the south bridge

00:01:24.096 --> 00:01:30.144
gets every unit there on time, with the same workers,
demand and deadlines.

00:01:30.144 --> 00:01:33.401
Waiting increases, so the tradeoff remains visible.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.704
Creators can design a different challenge: close the
north bridge from turn twenty-five

00:01:41.704 --> 00:01:47.408
through fifty. Muse Spark compiles that instruction
into a validated schedule. The bridge

00:01:47.408 --> 00:01:53.112
really closes, couriers reroute, and on-time delivery
falls from twenty to sixteen. This

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:57.500
is a creator design change, separate from a
same-resource repair.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.549
Under that new schedule, another recorded Meta
playtest improves delivery from sixteen to

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:09.098
twenty-two. The closure remains locked. Some
deadlines are still missed, and the app

00:02:09.098 --> 00:02:14.646
says so. A bounded search is useful evidence, not
proof that every level

00:02:14.646 --> 00:02:15.500
is solved.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.771
Save the world, checkpoints and trials as a portable
project. Reopening validates the

00:02:21.771 --> 00:02:27.542
inputs and recomputes the measurements. Export an
inspectable report or a smooth replay

00:02:27.542 --> 00:02:33.313
clip directly from the product. These local replays
need no API calls, so

00:02:33.313 --> 00:02:36.420
a collaborator can examine the same experiment.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.633
LiveWorld is a tabletop playtest studio for small
logistics games. It connects design

00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:51.267
intent to an executable system, a visible failure,
and a measured change. The

00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:56.900
prototype uses a fixed grid and two timed mechanics.
The next step is

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.500
testing this workflow with game creators.
